## Dropbox — never sync the `Old` folder

Every machine on this fleet (all four Linux boxes + the mac) is linked to Derek's
Dropbox account. **`Dropbox/Old` must stay excluded from selective sync on every one
of them, permanently.** It is ~411,000 files. On a freshly linked box it begins
downloading during the very first index — xavier took 2.0G in under a minute on
2026-08-19 and reported an ETA of "2+ days" before the exclusion landed.

**Rules — no exceptions:**

- **Never un-exclude it.** Do not run `dropbox.py exclude remove` against `Old`, and
  never "repair" a box by re-including it. If a task appears to need a file from
  `Old`, stop and ask Derek — pull that one file from the Dropbox web UI instead.
- **Never delete `~/Dropbox/Old` contents to reclaim disk.** A local delete syncs to
  the account and to every other machine. Excluding is the only correct move; if a
  box is short on space, the answer is elsewhere.
- **When linking a NEW box, exclude it the moment it appears:**
  ```bash
  python3 ~/bin/dropbox.py exclude add ~/Dropbox/Old
  ```
  `exclude add` **silently no-ops if the path does not exist locally yet**, so it
  cannot be pre-applied before linking. Watch for `~/Dropbox/Old` to appear during
  the first index, then exclude immediately — that window is about a minute.
- **Verify before trusting** any box:
  ```bash
  python3 ~/bin/dropbox.py exclude list     # must list Dropbox/Old
  ```

macOS runs the Dropbox desktop app rather than `dropbox.py` — the same rule applies
there through its Selective Sync preferences pane. As of 2026-08-19 `Old` is
correctly excluded on all five machines; that is the state to preserve.
